  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWith the north to northwesterly flow in place the pesky lake\neffect rain showers and drizzle will continue into this evening\nand areas near Lake Michigan may still see a few light rain\nshowers/sprinkles into late tonight as drier air is entrained on\nthe eastern side of the high amplitude ridging that is slowly\nmoving eastward into the region. However, lake effect clouds\nwill stick around through most of the day Sunday. Temperatures\non Sunday will remain cool with high temperatures remaining\nmostly in the mid to upper 40s with the clouds keeping solar\ninsolation in check.\n\nBut as the ridging continues to push eastward into the area a \ngradual warming trend will commence Monday onward with slight \nwarming each day along with decreasing cloud cover into the \nbeginning of the work week. Highs Monday through Wednesday will \nwarm into the 50s, and by Thursday high temperatures will get \nback into the low to mid 60s into the weekend. A bit of \nuncertainty in the longer term as guidance is rather quick to \nbreak down the high amplitude ridging over the central CONUS \nwhich may be too progressive with this type of pattern.",
